WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:03.870
-My next guest is a three-time
NFL Defensive Player of the Year

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.405
for the Houston Texans.

00:00:05.405 --> 00:00:07.406
He also does incredible work
through his charity,

00:00:07.406 --> 00:00:08.974
the J.J. Watt Foundation.

00:00:08.974 --> 00:00:11.310
Here he is, J.J. Watt!

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:14.614
Ah, come on. Look at you.
-Thank you, my friend.

00:00:14.614 --> 00:00:18.317
-Wow. Look at -- Look at --
Just surrounded by awards.

00:00:18.317 --> 00:00:21.421
-Yeah. They're all papier-mâché.
I made them myself.

00:00:21.421 --> 00:00:23.589
-Oh! Oh, my gosh.
Well, they look real.

00:00:23.589 --> 00:00:25.157
-Yeah, thank you.

00:00:25.157 --> 00:00:27.293
-The big NFL story
from this past weekend --

00:00:27.293 --> 00:00:30.129
Tom Brady's going back
to the Super Bowl.

00:00:30.129 --> 00:00:32.431
This time as quarterback
of Tampa Bay.

00:00:32.431 --> 00:00:34.367
Tampa Bay Buccaneers.

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:37.336
It's his 10th
Super Bowl appearance.

00:00:37.336 --> 00:00:40.606
So, to put this in context,
you were 12 years old

00:00:40.606 --> 00:00:44.410
when Tom Brady played
his first Super Bowl in 2002.

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:46.545
We have a photo of
what you looked like then.

00:00:46.545 --> 00:00:48.214
That was you
when he was in his first --

00:00:48.214 --> 00:00:49.748
-[ Laughs ] Oh, my gosh.

00:00:49.748 --> 00:00:52.318
-Look at that guy.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:00:52.318 --> 00:00:55.455
No, that was back when my mom
would put a bowl on my head

00:00:55.455 --> 00:00:57.157
to cut my hair.
That's how we did it.

00:00:57.157 --> 00:00:59.059
I don't know if anybody
does that anymore,

00:00:59.059 --> 00:01:00.860
but that was the thing
we did back then.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:03.229
-That was the way to do it.
A bowl haircut. Absolutely.

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:05.198
-Yeah. But Tom's unbelievable.

00:01:05.198 --> 00:01:07.833
I mean, it's incredible
what he's done. It's --

00:01:07.833 --> 00:01:10.203
I don't think people truly
understand or appreciate

00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:13.639
or can fathom
how insanely difficult it is.

00:01:13.639 --> 00:01:16.709
And the guy is just --
He is the greatest of all time.

00:01:16.709 --> 00:01:18.778
-I mean, you've gone up
against Brady a bunch of times.

00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:22.081
In fact, we have a photo of one
of the hits that you put on him.

00:01:22.081 --> 00:01:24.918
Look at this.
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:24.918 --> 00:01:26.419
-Look at the --
Zoom in at Brady's face.

00:01:26.419 --> 00:01:27.953
Can we get a close-up?
-Here we go.

00:01:27.953 --> 00:01:29.922
-He's like, "Ugh! Boy.

00:01:29.922 --> 00:01:32.291
This is gonna hurt
when I go down."

00:01:32.291 --> 00:01:34.193
-I'll sign that one
and send it to him,

00:01:34.193 --> 00:01:35.995
and I'll make sure
that he has that.

00:01:35.995 --> 00:01:37.830
He can hang that on his locker
right before he goes

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:40.966
and plays in the Super Bowl,
so...

00:01:40.966 --> 00:01:45.237
-This season obviously didn't go
as you hoped it would.

00:01:45.237 --> 00:01:47.106
Texas finished 4-12.

00:01:47.106 --> 00:01:49.041
And there was a postgame
press conference.

00:01:49.041 --> 00:01:51.044
The only reason
I'm bringing this up, J.J.,

00:01:51.044 --> 00:01:52.945
is because
there's a press conference

00:01:52.945 --> 00:01:54.747
that went viral a few weeks ago

00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:56.983
where you were talked about
how you were feeling.

00:01:56.983 --> 00:01:59.485
We have a clip.
Let's just take a look at this.

00:01:59.485 --> 00:02:02.488
-This is a job. We are getting
paid a whole lot of money.

00:02:02.488 --> 00:02:05.691
There are a lot of people that
watch us and invest their time

00:02:05.691 --> 00:02:07.360
and their money
into buying our jerseys

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:09.161
and buying a whole bunch
of [bleep].

00:02:09.161 --> 00:02:11.597
So if you can't go out there
and you can't work out,

00:02:11.597 --> 00:02:14.099
you can't show up on time,
you can't practice,

00:02:14.099 --> 00:02:17.103
you can't want to go out there
and win, you shouldn't be here!

00:02:17.103 --> 00:02:19.272
There are people every week
that still tweet you,

00:02:19.272 --> 00:02:21.140
that still come up to you
and say,

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:23.309
"Hey, we're still rooting for
you. We're still behind you."

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:25.778
They have no reason
whatsoever to. We stink!

00:02:25.778 --> 00:02:27.780
[ Sighs ]

00:02:30.149 --> 00:02:32.986
That's who I feel
the most bad for, is our fans.

00:02:32.986 --> 00:02:35.655
-Was that therapeutic?
-A little bit.

00:02:35.655 --> 00:02:39.859
You know, it was -- it was --
Any time an athlete is asked,

00:02:39.859 --> 00:02:41.494
you know,
"How do you get motivated

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:43.529
even though your team
is out of the playoffs?"

00:02:43.529 --> 00:02:45.865
Or, "How do you
still go out there

00:02:45.865 --> 00:02:47.866
and give it
everything you have?"

00:02:47.866 --> 00:02:51.070
It always frustrates me
because -- how can you not?

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:53.539
Like, I --
I'll never understand that.

00:02:53.539 --> 00:02:56.509
You know, I was a fan.
I grew up in Wisconsin.

00:02:56.509 --> 00:02:58.778
Where I obviously watched
the Green Bay Packers.

00:02:58.778 --> 00:03:01.080
Win, lose, or draw,
you were wearing that jersey

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:03.916
and you were watching the game.

00:03:03.916 --> 00:03:05.418
And I just --
I've never understood the fact

00:03:05.418 --> 00:03:07.653
that we're athletes getting paid
an immense amount of money

00:03:07.653 --> 00:03:10.189
to play a child's game,
and there's people all over

00:03:10.189 --> 00:03:12.658
that support us
and pour their energy in,

00:03:12.658 --> 00:03:15.160
and I'll never understand
how you could possibly

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:17.630
not give everything you got.
-There's a lot up in the air

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:19.298
about where the Texans go
from here.

00:03:19.298 --> 00:03:23.068
There are reports that your
quarterback, Deshaun Watson,

00:03:23.068 --> 00:03:25.104
has requested a trade.

00:03:25.104 --> 00:03:27.039
There's also speculation
that I heard

00:03:27.039 --> 00:03:28.708
that you could be traded.

00:03:28.708 --> 00:03:32.110
How do you process that kind of
chatter and those rumors?

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:35.481
And do you pay attention?

00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:37.817
-I process it
by going to Hawaii for a week,

00:03:37.817 --> 00:03:39.952
and I probably
should have stayed there.

00:03:39.952 --> 00:03:42.288
I probably should have stayed.
It was great.

00:03:42.288 --> 00:03:45.191
-Everyone's --
-Lava flows.

00:03:45.191 --> 00:03:47.460
-The word doesn't get to Hawaii?

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:49.595
-No. No. No. We --

00:03:49.595 --> 00:03:51.698
The word in Hawaii
was "manapua,"

00:03:51.698 --> 00:03:53.633
and we were enjoying mai tais
by the pool.

00:03:53.633 --> 00:03:56.335
-Yeah.
-So that was a good way for me

00:03:56.335 --> 00:04:00.005
to cope with it, and I might
just head back there.

00:04:00.005 --> 00:04:02.675
-Your two brothers
play for the Steelers.

00:04:02.675 --> 00:04:05.378
We've had them on the show.

00:04:05.378 --> 00:04:07.279
There are reports
that the Steelers are interested

00:04:07.279 --> 00:04:08.848
in trading for you.

00:04:08.848 --> 00:04:13.285
If that were the case, and
there's three Watts on one team,

00:04:13.285 --> 00:04:15.487
has that ever happened,
like, even back

00:04:15.487 --> 00:04:19.258
when you were on the Pirates or
even back in Pee Wee football?

00:04:19.258 --> 00:04:23.229
-No, no. Derek and T.J. have
played together in high school,

00:04:23.229 --> 00:04:25.297
college, and the NFL,
which is unbelievable,

00:04:25.297 --> 00:04:28.601
but we never all played
on the same team together.

00:04:28.601 --> 00:04:30.736
I'm not even sure
if it's physically possible

00:04:30.736 --> 00:04:33.072
just from
a bantering standpoint.

00:04:33.072 --> 00:04:36.509
Like, somebody would, like,
hide somebody's shoes.

00:04:36.509 --> 00:04:38.044
Just, like, childhood games.

00:04:38.044 --> 00:04:40.679
-Brothers.
-Yeah. Exactly.

00:04:40.679 --> 00:04:42.448
-Yeah.
-Not that we do that.

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:44.550
Not that we do that, Jimmy.
-No, no, no.

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:46.919
I had all three of you
on the show.

00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:48.421
We talked about it.

00:04:48.421 --> 00:04:51.290
I can see exactly
how much fun you have, man.

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:53.760
Hey, I want to talk
about the J.J. Watt Foundation

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:56.328
because this thing is fantastic.

00:04:56.328 --> 00:04:58.898
And you started this back
when you were in college.

00:04:58.898 --> 00:05:01.033
Can you talk about
what the foundation does

00:05:01.033 --> 00:05:03.536
and what inspired you
to start it?

00:05:03.536 --> 00:05:05.505
-Yeah, we started
back when I was in college.

00:05:05.505 --> 00:05:06.872
I appreciate you bringing it up.

00:05:06.872 --> 00:05:08.407
We started
when I was in college.

00:05:08.407 --> 00:05:10.876
I was inspired by Myron Rolle,
who was at Florida State

00:05:10.876 --> 00:05:14.346
and had his own foundation while
he was playing college football.

00:05:14.346 --> 00:05:16.048
I thought that was incredible,

00:05:16.048 --> 00:05:18.151
and it inspired me
to start my own.

00:05:18.151 --> 00:05:20.086
And one day I was
volunteering at a school,

00:05:20.086 --> 00:05:21.854
and the teacher
was telling me a story

00:05:21.854 --> 00:05:25.224
about how a child
robbed his neighbor

00:05:25.224 --> 00:05:26.792
with a screwdriver after school.

00:05:26.792 --> 00:05:29.929
I said, "Well, doesn't he have
anybody at home watching him?"

00:05:29.929 --> 00:05:31.597
And she said,
"He gets home at 3:00,

00:05:31.597 --> 00:05:33.565
and his mom doesn't get home
until 5:00."

00:05:33.565 --> 00:05:35.734
And I said, "Don't you guys
have sports or anything

00:05:35.734 --> 00:05:37.036
after school he can do?"

00:05:37.036 --> 00:05:39.572
They said, "Budget cuts
haven't allowed us

00:05:39.572 --> 00:05:41.540
to have any sports
after school."

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:44.910
And I just personally took that
as no child should ever suffer

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:46.712
because adults can't afford it.

00:05:46.712 --> 00:05:49.348
You know, us as adults, we have
to try and figure it all out.

00:05:49.348 --> 00:05:51.483
But kids --
kids have no control.

00:05:51.483 --> 00:05:54.286
They shouldn't be suffering
and lacking opportunities

00:05:54.286 --> 00:05:56.589
just because
we can't give them to them.

00:05:56.589 --> 00:05:58.256
So that's why I started
my foundation.

00:05:58.256 --> 00:06:00.760
We've done work in --

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:03.129
I believe it's over 33 states
in the country now

00:06:03.129 --> 00:06:05.164
we've donated to, and
there's schools and everything,

00:06:05.164 --> 00:06:07.833
and it's been awesome,
and it's been extremely --

00:06:07.833 --> 00:06:10.436
We have unbelievable fans
and volunteers who help us out.

00:06:10.436 --> 00:06:11.804
-It's so cool that you do that

00:06:11.804 --> 00:06:13.939
and that you give back
and make people happy.

00:06:13.939 --> 00:06:16.008
Congrats on that.
I always like to bring that up

00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:17.876
because it's really cool
that you do that.

00:06:17.876 --> 00:06:19.645
-I appreciate that
-J.J. Watt, everybody.

00:06:19.645 --> 00:06:21.748
For more information
on J.J.'s charity,

00:06:21.748 --> 00:06:25.151 line:0%
check out jjwfoundation.org.
-I appreciate you, my friend.

00:06:25.151 --> 00:06:27.220 line:0%
Thank you. Have a good week.
-Good to see you.

